{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7EbKjg6WQ25JewDzNLd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft this, a few questions. The answers will change what goes into the letter:\n\n1. **How did the 5-week absence end?** Did he come back on the date he said, and did you keep his job open for him? I'm thinking of mentioning it briefly and framing it as a family emergency he handled responsibly, but only if that matches what happened. Or would you rather leave it out completely?\n2. **How did he learn the herd management software?** Did he teach himself, did you train him, or did he ask to take it on? Also, did the vet or anyone else say anything about the 9 mastitis cases, like money or cows saved? A specific result like that would be the strongest line in the letter.\n3. **Do you have a sense of what he wants to do after the program?** For example, come back to your farm, move into herd health, or run his own operation. Would you hire him again or promote him when he finishes?\n4. **Should the letter mention his written English and the night class?** The admissions committee may see it in his application essay anyway. If you bring it up first and point to the class, it reads as effort, not a weakness. But it's your call.\n5. **Details for the header and signature:** your name, the farm's name and location, Tomás's last name, and the college's name. Is there a specific person or committee to address it to?\n\nIf you don't know some of these, just say so. I'll write without them and won't fill in anything you haven't told me."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":377,"cache_read_input_tokens":9513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":377},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnost